.TH CLEANER 1 "2026-07-12" "linux-cleaner 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cleaner \- safely clean cache and temporary files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cleaner
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cleaner
is a configuration-driven Bash utility that estimates and reclaims disk space by
cleaning caches, temporary files, and logs. It runs in dry-run mode by default,
routes every deletion through a path-safety check, blocks system directories and
paths outside the user's
.BR $HOME ,
refuses to follow symlinks, and can move files to a restorable quarantine instead
of deleting them permanently.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Use a custom configuration file instead of the resolved default.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry\-run
Calculate and display potential space savings without deleting anything (default).
.TP
.B \-\-clean
Perform cleanup with a summary confirmation prompt.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Prompt for confirmation before cleaning each target.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Clean immediately without confirmation prompts (except danger level).
.TP
.B \-\-yes\-danger
Bypass the manual confirmation prompt for danger-risk targets.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-trash
Move files to quarantine instead of permanent deletion.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-risk " " \fILEVEL\fR
Maximum risk level to clean: safe, medium, or danger (default: safe).
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-exclude " " \fIPATTERNS\fR
Comma-separated glob patterns to exclude from cleanup.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-outside\-home
Explicitly allow cleaning targets outside the user's
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.B \-\-log \fR[\fIFILE\fR]
Append an operation log (opt-in, off by default; kept under
.BR $HOME ).
.TP
.B \-\-list\-sessions
List quarantine sessions (read-only) and exit. Each session is shown with its
timestamp, size, and date, followed by the targets it holds: the type
.RB ( full
or
.BR aged )
and original path of every quarantined item, so a session's contents can be
inspected before it is restored or permanently purged.
.TP
.B \-\-restore \fISESSION\fR
Restore files from a quarantine session (latest or a timestamp).
.TP
.B \-\-purge\-sessions \fISESSION\fR
Permanently delete quarantine session(s) to reclaim space:
.IR latest ", " all ", or a specific timestamp."
Irreversible; prompts for confirmation unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the tool version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display the usage message and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Targets are pipe-delimited lines:
.PP
.nf
RISK_LEVEL|NAME|PATH_EXPRESSION|DESCRIPTION|MIN_AGE_DAYS|CHECK_PROCESSES|CUSTOM_ACTION
.fi
.PP
The first five fields are required; the last two are optional. A malformed line is
rejected with a line-numbered error. The default config is resolved from an explicit
.BR \-c ,
then the XDG user config, then
.IR /etc/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf ,
then a
.I cleaner.conf
beside the script.
.SH SAFETY
Deletions are blocked for the filesystem root, system directories
.RI ( /usr ", " /etc ", " /var ", " /lib ", "
and similar), and any path resolving outside
.BR $HOME
unless
.B \-\-allow\-outside\-home
is given. Symlinked targets are refused, and paths are physically canonicalized to
prevent traversal or symlink escapes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf
XDG user configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf
System configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/quarantine/
Quarantine sessions for
.BR \-\-trash / \-\-restore / \-\-purge\-sessions .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/operations.log
Default operation log (only when
.B \-\-log
is enabled).
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and non-zero on a configuration error or a failed operation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview what would be cleaned:
.B cleaner
.TP
Clean safe targets, moving files to quarantine:
.B cleaner \-\-trash \-\-clean
.TP
List and restore quarantined files:
.B cleaner \-\-list\-sessions
.br
.B cleaner \-\-restore latest
.TP
Reclaim space by purging all quarantine sessions:
.B cleaner \-\-purge\-sessions all \-\-force
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR paccache (8),
.BR find (1)
